---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';

const page = {
  title: "Write a Raspberry Pi image to an SD card — Bootable",
  description: "Find, download, write, and verify Raspberry Pi IMG and compressed disk images on removable SD cards and USB media.",
  ogTitle: "Write a Raspberry Pi image to an SD card",
  ogDescription: "Catalog downloads, compressed images, expanded-size checks, and read-back verification.",
  ogType: "article",
  current: "guide",
  doc: { label: "Safety", href: "https://github.com/debpalash/bootable/blob/main/docs/safety.md" },
  crumbs: [{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Guides", href: "/guides.html" }, { label: "Raspberry Pi image" }],
};
---
<Layout {...page}>
<main class="guide-main">
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={page.crumbs} />
      <article>
        <header class="guide-hero"><p class="eyebrow">Raspberry Pi · IMG · SD</p><h1>Write a Raspberry Pi image to an SD card.</h1><p class="lede">Browse Pi images or open a local IMG, write the expanded bytes, and verify the target before removing it.</p></header>
        <div class="guide-body">
          <div class="guide-content">
            <h2 id="source">Choose a Raspberry Pi image</h2>
            <p>Open the Raspberry Pi catalog in Bootable, search the available images, and queue a download. You can pause, resume, cancel, or retry the job. A local <code>.img</code>, <code>.raw</code>, or supported compressed file works through the same inspection path.</p>
            <p>When a publisher checksum file is available, Bootable verifies the downloaded file before allowing it into a write plan. The interface distinguishes a verified checksum from an HTTPS-only download.</p>

            <h2 id="capacity">Check the real capacity</h2>
            <p>Compressed files can be much smaller than the disk image they contain. Bootable measures the full expanded length before review and checks that the expanded image fits the card.</p>
            <p>Bootable also provides destructive bad-block and fake-capacity tests. Run those only on expendable media; the test itself overwrites the selected device.</p>

            <h2 id="steps">Write the card</h2>
            <ol>
              <li>Choose a catalog image or local file.</li>
              <li>Insert the SD card through a removable or USB-attached reader.</li>
              <li>Select the whole card, not a mounted partition.</li>
              <li>Review the expanded size, physical target, and permanent erasure.</li>
              <li>Write and wait for read-back verification.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="guide-callout"><p><strong>Check the adapter and card.</strong> The selected target is the entire removable device. Existing partitions and files will be erased.</p></div>

            <h2 id="verification">What gets compared</h2>
            <p>For compressed media, Bootable hashes the expanded stream sent to the card. It then reads the same byte range back from the card and compares SHA-256. Verification covers the written bytes, not whether a specific Pi model supports the image.</p>

            <h2 id="after">After the write</h2>
            <p>Wait for the safe-removal result before disconnecting the reader. If the write was cancelled or the verification failed, rewrite the entire image. Do not treat an interrupted card as bootable.</p>

            <section class="related-guides"><h2>Related guides</h2><ul><li><a href="/compressed-image-writer">How compressed image streaming works</a></li><li><a href="/verify-bootable-usb">Read-back verification explained</a></li><li><a href="/write-iso-to-usb">Write an ISO to USB</a></li></ul></section>
            <div class="guide-cta"><h2>Use Bootable for SD media</h2><p>Choose the native desktop app or mouse-enabled terminal interface.</p><a class="button primary" href="/download">Download Bootable</a></div>
          </div>
          <aside class="guide-aside"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li><a href="#source">Choose an image</a></li><li><a href="#capacity">Capacity checks</a></li><li><a href="#steps">Write the card</a></li><li><a href="#verification">Verification</a></li><li><a href="#after">After the write</a></li></ul></aside>
        </div>
      </article>
</main>
</Layout>
